implementationPlan.what:
Owner review.

Cherry-pick the B commits onto design-v5 in the order B1 to B6, then run npm run ship-check. With the preview running, run npm run screenshots: 360, 768 and 1280 in light, plus the *_dark-os captures.

Show Rajvir the ten decisions in those screenshots:
- the white page under his Mac's dark mode;
- the one blue bar with the white-disc badge;
- ink headings;
- ink list rows;
- blue only in its five jobs;
- no gold;
- one typeface;
- the lean lesson first screen with 'In this lesson' folded on a phone and in the rail on a laptop;
- no motion;
- the neutral secondary buttons.

Ask the open questions: the shorter consent wording, adding an Atkinson TTF for share images, and the CLAUDE.md and .claude edits. Record his answers in the step 24 commit. Do not delete anything old or rewrite the docs before his yes. If he changes a decision, loop back to the step that owns it.

Final gate and reviews. Run npm run ship-check and npm run screenshots. Ask for verdicts from:
- design-reviewer (tokens only, one primary per view, blue in its five jobs, no cards except figure boxes, light only);
- a11y-perf-auditor (the design.spec results, 320 and 640x400 reflow, forced colours, the JS budget);
- security-reviewer (the CSP is unchanged: no style attribute, no injected <style>, no new origin; the header script and LessonScript grew by a few hundred bytes);
- content-reviewer (the /accessibility wording only).

Put the verdicts in the commit messages. Small commits on design-v5. Never push, merge or deploy; Rajvir does that.

